<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Danish India</h1><p class="st-meta">1620 CE – 1869 CE · capital Tranquebar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Denmark's trading posts in India, led by Tranquebar on the Coromandel Coast, where the first Protestant missionaries in India began work in 1706.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A Danish expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ove-gjedde">Ove Gjedde</a> reached south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and in 1620 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghunatha-nayak">Raghunatha Nayak</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-east-india-company">Danish East India Company</a> the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> (Tharangambadi). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-dansborg">Fort Dansborg</a> there, which became the centre of their Asian trade.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was governed by the company and later the crown, trading in textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>. In 1706 the German missionaries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartholomaus-ziegenbalg">Bartholomäus Ziegenbalg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-plutschau">Heinrich Plütschau</a>, sent by the Danish king, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tranquebar-mission">Tranquebar Mission</a> and translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> into Tamil. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serampore">Serampore</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, acquired in 1755, became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serampore-college">Serampore College</a>, founded by English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptists">Baptists</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> also claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicobar-islands">Nicobar Islands</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Overshadowed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British India</a> and loss-making, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serampore">Serampore</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1845 and gave up its claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicobar-islands">Nicobar Islands</a> in 1868-1869. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-dansborg">Fort Dansborg</a> and Tranquebar's churches still stand.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/danish-india.jpg" alt="Danish India" width="320" height="168"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naval_Ensign_of_Denmark.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1620 CE – 1869 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tranquebar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ove Gjedde</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Danish, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tranquebar, Danish East India, Dansk Ostindien</dd></dl></aside></div>

